BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Clay Exploration Evenings: Lvl 2\, 7/16 - 8/20 — Sebastopol Cent
 er For the Arts\, California
DTSTART:20260716T000000
DTEND:20260717T000000
UID:9845642f70082863939973e15798e3c7@sebarts.org
DESCRIPTION:Clay Exploration Evenings: Lvl 2\, 7/16 - 8/20\n\n  \n    \n  
     \n        \n          $255.00\n        \n      \n    \n  \n\nThursdays
 \, July 16 - August 20\, 2026 (6 sessions) 6 - 8:30pm$210 + $45 clay fee (
 pre-registration required)This course is designed for students wishing to 
 explore hand-building techniques at an intermediate to advanced level and 
 delve deeper into refining their clay skills. Each session will start with
  a demonstration of a specific technique related to functional and/or deco
 rative pottery. Participants are invited to explore new ideas and to try p
 ieces that might be of a more challenging nature. Some of what we can expl
 ore are freeform coil pots\, soft slab work\, hard slab technique\, a vari
 ety of sculptural techniques and a sampling of the many stamping\, carving
  and textural surface treatments on clay. We will investigate ways to use 
 slips and glaze that will add color to the forms we create.About the Instr
 uctor: Michael Citrino has had a love affair with clay for over 50 years. 
 After his college experience at California College of Arts and Crafts with
  Viola Frey and further work at Humboldt State University with Lew Merrick
 \, he ran a stained glass business for 18 years. Michael and his wife left
  for Turkey in 1991 to teach in International Schools. For 26 years he tau
 ght K-12 art\, including Ceramics\, Drawing\, Photography\, Design and Scu
 lpture at schools in six different countries. He started clay programs for
  High School and Middle School students at three of those schools. At four
  of these schools he offered after school and weekend ceramics sessions fo
 r teachers and parents. Michael’s work ranges from the traditional to th
 e experimental and a wide swath in between. His approach to teaching is to
  help students explore the playful nature of the clay room and nurture a l
 ove for the possibilities that medium presents. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n
 \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n          \n            \n        
